Title: 0003-construct-simsprite
Repository: micropoliscore
Base-Commit: 69ccf87ed72d2d2950b70cf16ef1391439bd9d48
Requires: 0002-voteproblems-off-by-one.patch
Author: aros-micropolis project
Date: 2026-09-15
Upstream-Status: candidate
Scope: generic - any native platform with libstdc++; hidden in WASM by zeroed heap

Problem:
  SimSprite contains a std::string member, but newSprite() allocated it with newPtr() (plain malloc), then immediately assigned sprite->name. An unconstructed std::string is undefined behavior on any platform: it 'worked' in WASM because a freshly zeroed heap happens to form a valid empty libc++ string; on dirty malloc memory (host, AROS) it crashed with SEGV in string assign (host ASan run, sprite.cpp:117 via generatePlane -> makeSprite -> newSprite during loadFile). Additionally spriteList/freeSprites/globalSprites[] are read (pool walk, getSprite) before any assignment, so uninitialized values send the pool down a garbage pointer.
Solution:
  Construct SimSprite properly with 'new SimSprite()' (value-initialization also zeroes the POD members; sprites are pooled and never individually freed, so no matching delete is needed). Initialize spriteList/freeSprites in the constructor initializer list and zero the globalSprites[] array in the constructor body.
Validation:
  Host: ASan build (build/host/harness), 192 phases of haight.cty, clean
  (2026-09-15). Guest: AROS One 1.3 (ABIv11) under QEMU/TCG, GCC 10.5.0,
  192 phases, clean exit, RESULT cityTime=4851 year=2001 month=0.
  Scope: headless
  harness only - no UI, no scenario path, no save.
Removal:
  Fill in when this is a workaround; permanent fixes say so here.

diff --git a/packages/micropolis-engine/src/micropolis.cpp b/packages/micropolis-engine/src/micropolis.cpp
index 63925da..e0024b2 100644
--- a/packages/micropolis-engine/src/micropolis.cpp
+++ b/packages/micropolis-engine/src/micropolis.cpp
@@ -114,8 +114,19 @@ Micropolis::Micropolis() :
         // as callback, same crash on dirty malloc memory.
         mapBase(0),
         mopBase(0),
-        historyInitialized(false)
+        historyInitialized(false),
+        // Sprite bookkeeping is read (getSprite/newSprite walk these) before
+        // anything assigns them; uninitialized values send the pool down a
+        // garbage pointer. Same zeroed-heap luck as the other members.
+        spriteList(0),
+        freeSprites(0)
 {
+    // globalSprites[] is an array - zero it in the body, the initializer
+    // list cannot.
+    for (int i = 0; i < SPRITE_COUNT; i++) {
+        globalSprites[i] = (SimSprite *)NULL;
+    }
+
     initMapArrays();
 }
 
diff --git a/packages/micropolis-engine/src/sprite.cpp b/packages/micropolis-engine/src/sprite.cpp
index 5cab2c8..884d5dd 100644
--- a/packages/micropolis-engine/src/sprite.cpp
+++ b/packages/micropolis-engine/src/sprite.cpp
@@ -111,7 +111,14 @@ SimSprite *Micropolis::newSprite(const std::string &name, int type, int x, int y
         sprite = freeSprites;
         freeSprites = sprite->next;
     } else {
-        sprite = (SimSprite *)newPtr(sizeof (SimSprite));
+        // SimSprite contains a std::string, so raw malloc memory is not a
+        // SimSprite: assigning sprite->name below would operate on an
+        // unconstructed string object. A freshly zeroed WASM heap happens
+        // to form a valid empty libc++ string, which is why the browser
+        // build never crashed; libstdc++ (AROS) has no such luck. Only a
+        // real constructor makes this defined behavior. Sprites are pooled
+        // and never individually freed, so there is no matching delete.
+        sprite = new SimSprite();
     }
 
     sprite->name = name;
